ump-aligned super PAC activity in this swing seat justify trimming the margin a few points. I land at a 7.5-point Democratic edge, still comfortably Likely D.", "watch": "A late wave of Republican outside spending or polling showing O'Donnell closing within single digits would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Susie Lee (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Susie Lee, a two-term incumbent in a Las Vegas-area district that leans only about a point Democratic, is seeking reelection against Republican Martin O'Donnell, a video game composer who has self-funded a competitive campaign. Both sides have raised over $4 million, though Lee holds a cash-on-hand edge. No independent candidates are factors.\n\nRaters split between Lean D and Likely D, and prediction markets give Lee roughly a 92 to 94 percent chance of winning. Republicans have made the seat a priority, aided by Trump's 'no tax on tips' policy and super PAC spending, which argues for a somewhat narrower margin than fundamentals alone would suggest. With no public polling available, the forecast relies on fundamentals, ratings, and markets, keeping uncertainty relatively wide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9366, "margin": 9.91}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8673, "margin": 7.5}]}